{"actor":{"id":"claude-fable-5","type":"agent"},"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"**Result: inconclusive — the free tier can't run Codex at all.** Every request (including a clean control session with no injected reasoning) got the same 400 — \"the model is not supported when using Codex with a ChatGPT account\" — across `gpt-5.6-sol`, `gpt-5.6-codex`, `gpt-5.6-codex-mini`, `codex-mini-latest`, and `gpt-5.2-codex`. The API rejected everything at the entitlement layer, so the encrypted reasoning item was never actually evaluated. We learned nothing about the encryption keying either way. Both roadmaps are updated with the attempt and its outcome (committed).\n\n**You can log Codex back into your main account now.**\n\nTo actually settle the question, the second identity needs real Codex access. Two routes:\n\n1. **A second paid ChatGPT account** — anyone with Plus; if you ever bridge sessions with a collaborator, that's a two-minute test on their machine: sync the ledger (or just copy the donor rollout), run `node scripts/probe-encrypted-reasoning.mjs <donor>`, and read pass/400.\n2. **API-key auth on a different platform org** — Codex supports API-key login, and platform orgs are separate identities from ChatGPT accounts. If you have (or spin up) a second org with a few cents of billing, `codex login` with that key on this machine reproduces the cross-org condition without touching your main login. This is probably the cheapest self-serve path.\n\nUntil then the honest status is: same-account replay proven (cross-session, cross-CLI-version), cross-account unknown — which is exactly what the roadmaps now say. Notably, even the pessimistic outcome (per-account keying) wouldn't kill the feature: your own multi-machine resume — the same-account case — is already the proven half, and that alone justifies cledger preserving the blobs.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"main","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/repos/evintunador/turnbridge","head":"a61d7e48f18594b